-- Bitemporal RECALL x per-day interval iterators — DECIDED SEMANTICS
-- (smucclaw/l4-ide#914 §2B/§2C). The interval builtins (EVER BETWEEN etc.)
-- stamp the fact-time axis per stepped day, and since #914 RECALL reads
-- through that axis: a predicate RECALLing a cell is evaluated against the
-- ledger AS OF EACH ITERATION DAY. A contemporaneous write (valid-from =
-- the harness clock's day, 2025-01-31) is therefore NOT visible on 2020
-- iteration days, but IS visible in a window covering its valid-from day.
-- This is the per-day regime selection that makes e.g. Housing Act Ground 8
-- ("arrears at notice AND at hearing") expressible — the read-side twin of
-- the interval builtins' RULES EFFECTIVE DATE stamping.
--
-- Harness clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z.

IMPORT prelude

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`x is set on` MEANS fromMaybe 0 (RECALL `x`) GREATER THAN 0

-- expected: LIST 1, 200, 100 — the write is valid from 2025-01-31, so a
-- January-2020 window never sees it (the per-day RECALL yields NOTHING on
-- every stepped day: EVER -> FALSE -> 200) while a window covering
-- 2025-01-31 does (EVER -> TRUE -> 100). Each stepped day is its own
-- temporal scope for the predicate's RECALL.
#EVAL LIST (RECORD `x` IS 1), (IF `EVER BETWEEN` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020) (DATE_FROM_DMY 3 1 2020) `x is set on` THEN 100 ELSE 200), (IF `EVER BETWEEN` (DATE_FROM_DMY 30 1 2025) (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 2 2025) `x is set on` THEN 100 ELSE 200)
